 A small TypeScript library, with no runtime dependency, of building blocks for our services that store,
 exchange and update JSON: configuration files, API payloads and stored records. It is imported by the
 other projects of the team, so its behaviour must follow the published standards it implements.
+
+## JSON Pointer (RFC 6901)
+
+```ts
+import { resolvePointer, parsePointer, JsonPointerError } from "./src/index.ts";
+
+resolvePointer({ foo: ["bar", "baz"], "a/b": 1 }, "/foo/0"); // "bar"
+resolvePointer({ foo: ["bar", "baz"], "a/b": 1 }, "/a~1b");  // 1
+resolvePointer({ foo: ["bar", "baz"] }, "");                  // the whole document
+parsePointer("/a~1b/m~0n");                                   // ["a/b", "m~n"]
+```
+
+- `resolvePointer(document, pointer)` returns the value `pointer` refers to.
+- `parsePointer(pointer)` returns the decoded reference tokens.
+- Both throw a `JsonPointerError` (with the offending `pointer`) when the pointer is invalid: not empty
+  and not starting with `/`, or with a `~` not followed by `0` or `1`. `resolvePointer` also throws it
+  when the pointer does not resolve: a missing member, an array index that is malformed (`01`, `-1`)
+  or out of range, the `-` index (it names the element after the last one, which does not exist), or a
+  token applied to a primitive value.
+- Pointers are taken in their JSON string form (`/foo`), not their URI fragment form (`#/foo`).
+- Only the document's own members are reachable: `/__proto__`, `/constructor` or an array's `/length`
+  do not resolve.
+
+Run the tests with `npm test` (Node.js 22.18 or later, which runs TypeScript directly).
