+
+# JSON Pointer: strict RFC 6901 resolution semantics
+
+## Context
+
+`resolvePointer(document, pointer)` (task-001) must follow RFC 6901, since other team projects import
+this library. The RFC leaves a few points to the application: how an unresolvable pointer is handled
+(section 7), what the `-` array token means outside of JSON Patch, and it offers two representations
+of a pointer (JSON string, section 5; URI fragment, section 6). JavaScript objects also expose
+inherited properties (`__proto__`, `constructor`, an array's `length`) that are not JSON values.
+
+## Decision
+
+- Accept only the JSON string representation (`""` or starting with `/`); `#/foo` is invalid.
+- Reject any `~` not followed by `0` or `1`; decode `~1` before `~0`.
+- An array token must match `0 / [1-9][0-9]*` and be below the array length; `-` (the element after
+  the last), leading zeros, signs, decimals and exponents are errors.
+- On objects, any token (including `01` or `-`) is a member name; only own properties resolve.
+- Every failure, syntax or resolution, throws a `JsonPointerError`.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Pointers can never reach prototype members, so a pointer from an untrusted source cannot read
+  `__proto__`/`constructor` or anything that is not part of the JSON document.
+- Later features that need `-` (JSON Patch `add` at the array end) must handle that token themselves
+  instead of calling `resolvePointer` on it.
+- Callers holding a URI fragment must percent-decode it and strip the `#` before calling.
